ull,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/883690","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/883690"}},{"id":"877439","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877717","description":"Just after \"Big Balls\" is \"Slap Your Mama\", the second largest rapid on the run. Boof a ledge right of center (or far left) and then slide. For early take out follow a stream up the left side to the dirt logging road. Or continue a few more miles through boulder gardens becoming flat water to Blanchard","caption":"\"slap your mama\"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Tom Perkins","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/877439","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/877439"}},{"id":"9181","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6614","description":"For the full-sized version of this picture, <a href=\"http://www.americanwhitewater.org/photos/?photoid=6364\">click here</a>.","caption":"East Branch Piscataquis","poi_name":"","subject":"Anna Staehli","author":"Chris Hull","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/9181","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/9181"}},{"id":"877362","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877598","description":"We call this the \"Crack Drop\". Start right and drive left to avoid the right wall","caption":"Crack drop","poi_name":"Crack drop","subject":"","author":"Tom Perkins","poi_id":"103007","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/877362","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/877362"}},{"id":"877364","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877600","description":"\"Big Balls\" The largest rapid on the run. A 20 footer with a re-connect halfway down. Keep momentum and angle going left to avoid the right wall.","caption":"Big Balls","poi_name":"Big Balls","subject":"","author":"Tom Perkins","poi_id":"103008","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/877364","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/877364"}},{"id":"877365","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877601","description":"Christian in \"Big Balls\"","caption":"Christian in \"Big Balls\"","poi_name":"Big Balls","subject":"","author":"Tom Perkins","poi_id":"103008","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/877365","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/877365"}}],"detail":{"id":"871","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"Source: Greg and Sue Hanlon's [Steep Creeks of New England](http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1889787051/americanwhite-20), which has more info on this run. Text used with permission.\n\n**Directions:** From Greenville, head South on Rte. 6/15 about 6 miles to Upper Shirley Corner. Bang a right onto Shirley Mills Rd. and continue 1.4 miles to Shirley Pond. The pond is on the right; the dam, on the left. Put in below the dam on river left.\n\nTo takeout: head back toward 6/15. About 50 yards from the dam, take your first right onto an old railroad bed / logging road. Drive about 2.3 miles to a small stream crossing under the road and a cabin on the left. The low-water takeout is where this stream meets the East Branch. To get to the high-water takeout, which adds 1.3 miles to the trip, continue on the logging road and take a right down a steep washed-out logging road. Park and take out in the clearing where a small trail leads to the river.\n\n**UPDATED DIRECTIONS:** The logging road (B&A Railroad Road) and North Blanchard/Shirly Road are rarely passable in the Spring when the Piscataquis is running, due to poor road conditions, snow mounds and gates.  Hiking out through the snow and mud is long and tedious, though possible.  Instead of hiking out or getting stuck in the mud, drive to the center of Blanchard via route 15 through Monson.  Takeout at the bridge in the center of town (Blanchard Road).  There is a long flatwater paddle to get to Blanchard, but if you prefer to ride your boat rather than have your boat ride you, this is the way to go.  Put-in is still below the dam at Shirley Lake.  [Here is a GoogleMap](https://www.google.com/maps/dir/45.36608,-69.6203349/45.2674028,-69.5836909/@45.311027,-69.5786185,12z/data=!4m14!4m13!1m10!3m4!1m2!1d-69.6018333!2d45.35904!3s0x4cb06607fd9df2f1:0x88a16dd7a5d9fa3b!3m4!1m2!1d-69.5270152!2d45.3091298!3s0x4cb0697f5e384789:0xdfc1d51c33296163!1m0!3e0?hl=en).\n\n**Note:** The first complete run of the East Pis was made in 1992 by Scott Murray, Greg Hanlon, Bill and Joan Hildreth.\n\n**River Description by Nate Warren:** Best run with more than 1000 CFS on the Piscataquis Gauge.  The first few miles is filled with great class IV+ ledge drops with plenty of good boofs.  The aptly named Crack Drop (V-) is towards the end of this section.  It can be boat scouted from the pool.  Drive left.\n\nSoon below is Big Balls Falls (5.1), a 20 foot drop followed by a 5'er that gets sticky at higher flows.  The river turns 90 degrees left above the drop.  You can scout from either side, but walking is easier on the right (though not easy).  The main drop has been run almost everywhere.  I prefer center right working left to avoid a bouncy reconnect in the middle.\n\nThere is a small class 3-4 rapid below Big Balls, then the entrance to Slap Ya Mama (5.1).  This is the hardest rapid on the river.  Scout and portage on the left.  The most common line is down the center spout with a little left angle - boofing will hurt, as will pitoning.  Hold on down the slide and catch the eddy on the left to watch your friends.  If you are hiking out, start here.\n\nBelow slap is a bit of wood choked class 3-4, eventually petering down to the long flatwater paddle out to Blanchard.","averageGradient":"105","maxGradient":null,"length":"2.6","correlations":[{"reachID":"871","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"900","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"4000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"Piscataquis River at Blanchard, Maine","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"01031300","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"251","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"4.9","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"45.2672222","longitude":"-69.58388889"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/01031300","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/01031300/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":"Previous Flowbands: \nRange of Boatable Flows (R0-R9): 900 - 4000 cfs  Comment: High end flows are unknown.  This gets into serious class 5 at higher flows.\n\nMigration Errors: \nSingle range is valid but doesn't provide low/medium/high runnable\nThere is one comment for all of low/medium/high runnable.","comment":"The gauge is downstream and is a good correlation of flows.  It measures the combined East & West Pis, which can vary significantly as the West is swamp fed an","status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"251","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["111595","103008","116867","103009"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-69.62031929732679,45.366069157519135],[-69.62024586904244,45.365790596249894],[-69.620086202376,45.3